by 10 is 42. Therefore, he needs 42 pages.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't skip any steps. Let me recalculate the total cards. 60 packs, each 7 cards. So 7 * 60. Hmm, 7 times 60. Let me break it down: 7 times 60 is the same as 7 times 6 times 10. 7 times 6 is 42, so 42 times 10 is 420. Yeah, that's correct. So total cards is 420. Divided by 10 per page is 42. So, 42 pages. That seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can think of it as 60 packs times 7 cards per pack divided by 10 cards per page. So (60 * 7) / 10. Which is same as 420 / 10 = 42. Yep, same result.\n\nAnother way to check: 42 pages would hold 42 * 10 = 420 cards. Exactly what he needs. So that makes sense. There's no need for extra pages because there's no remainder. So the answer should be 42.\n\nI think that's it. Let me just recap to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. Total cards: 60 *7=420. Pages needed: 420 /10=42. Yep. So the final answer is 42 pages. So boxed, it would be \\boxed{42}. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of pages Punger needs to buy is \\boxed{42}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many pages Punger needs to buy the baseball cards, we follow a step-by-step process that ensures accuracy and clarity.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Calculate Total Number of Cards**\n\nPunger buys 60 packs of baseball cards, each pack containing 7 cards.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total cards} = 60 \\times 7 = 420\n$$\n\nSo, there are 420 cards in total.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Determine Number of Pages Needed**\n\nEach page can hold 10 cards. Since it's necessary to have full pages, we must round up if there is a remainder. Here, since 420 is a multiple of 10, there is no remainder, and thus:\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of pages} = \\frac{420}{10} = 42\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer**\n\nThe number of pages Punger needs to buy is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{42}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?", "answer": "60 packs of cards * 7 cards per pack = <<60*7=420>>420 cards\n420 cards / 10 cards per page = <<420/10=42>>42 pages\n#### 42", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["42"], "solution": "42"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30436, 1389, 51207, 19507, 7411, 13, 220, 1260, 49531, 220, 21, 15, 30306, 315, 19507, 7411, 13, 8886, 3769, 702, 220, 22, 7411, 4766, 13, 1260, 6801, 311, 2182, 1493, 7411, 304, 3281, 6816, 311, 6016, 279], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 30436, 1389, 49531, 220, 21, 15, 30306, 315, 19507, 7411, 11, 1817, 3769, 702, 220, 22, 7411, 11, 323, 566, 6801, 311, 2182, 1105, 389, 3281, 6816, 13, 8886, 2150, 9982, 220, 16, 15, 7411, 13, 358, 1184, 311, 1477, 700, 1246, 1657, 6816, 566, 3880, 13, 88190, 11, 773, 279, 3491, 374, 10161, 369, 279]}
