fore, on Monday, he reads one book (the first one) with 20 pages. Then Tuesday, reads the second book with 12 pages, and Wednesday, the third book. \n\nSo total pages from Monday to Wednesday: 20 (Mon) + 12 (Tue) + x (Wed) = 51.\n\nBut let me check if the problem is saying that he borrowed 3 books, and on each day he read one book. So that makes sense. So total pages read from Mon to Wed: 20 + 12 + x = 51. Therefore, x is 51 minus 20 minus 12. Let me calculate that.\n\n51 minus 20 is 31. Then 31 minus 12 is 19. Therefore, he read 19 pages on Wednesday. \n\nWait, that seems straightforward. Let me make sure I didn't miss anything. Let me check again:\n\nMonday: 20 pages.\n\nTuesday: 12 pages.\n\nWednesday: ?\n\nTotal: 20 + 12 + x = 51 => x = 51 - 20 -12 = 19. Yep, that's 19. \n\nIs there any possibility that the 20 and 12 are not added together? For example, maybe he read the first book twice, but the problem says he \"reads the first book with a total of 20 pages.\" So it's one book, so adding them together. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a trick here. Let me check again. Wait, he borrowed 3 books on Monday. Maybe during Monday, he borrowed 3 books, and then read them. But the problem says \"On Monday, he reads the first book... on Tuesday, he reads the second book...\". So maybe on Monday, he only read the first book, on Tuesday the second, and on Wednesday, the third. Therefore, the total pages added up are 20 + 12 + x. \n\nTherefore, solving for x, it's definitely 51 - 20 -12 = 19. So the answer should be 19 pages on Wednesday.\n\nI don't think I made any errors here. Maybe another way to verify: 20 + 12 is 32. So if the total is 51, then 51 minus 32 is 19. Yep, same result. So I think that's correct. \n\n**Final Answer**\nNico read \\boxed{19} pages on Wednesday.\n</think>\n\nTo find out how many pages Nico read on Wednesday, we start by summing the pages he read over the three days:\n\n- On **Monday**, he read **20 pages**.\n- On **Tuesday**, he read **12 pages**.\n- On **Wednesday**, we are to determine the number of pages he read, denoted by $ x $.\n\nThe total number of pages read over these three days is given as **51**. Therefore, we set up the equation:\n\n$$\n20 + 12 + x = 51\n$$\n\nSimplifying the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n32 + x = 51\n$$\n\nSolving for $ x $:\n\n$$\nx = 51 - 32 = 19\n$$\n\nThus, Nico read **19 pages on Wednesday**.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{19}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Nico borrows 3 books from the library on Monday.  On Monday, he reads the first book with a total of 20 pages.  On Tuesday, he reads the second book with a total of 12 pages.  On Wednesday, he reads the third book.  If he has read a total of 51 pages from Monday to Wednesday, how many pages did he read on Wednesday?", "answer": "From Monday to Tuesday, Nico reads a total of 20 + 12 = <<20+12=32>>32 pages.\nOn Wednesday, he read a total of 51 - 32 = <<51-32=19>>19 pages.\n#### 19", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Nico borrows 3 books from the library on Monday.  On Monday, he reads the first book with a total of 20 pages.  On Tuesday, he reads the second book with a total of 12 pages.  On Wednesday, he reads the third book.  If he has read a total of 51 pages from Monday to Wednesday, how many pages did he read on Wednesday?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["19"], "solution": "19"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 74035, 31632, 1811, 220, 18, 6467, 504, 279, 6733, 389, 7014, 13, 220, 1913, 7014, 11, 566, 15804, 279, 1156, 2311, 448, 264, 2790, 315, 220, 17, 15, 6816, 13, 220, 1913, 7589, 11, 566, 15804, 279], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 975, 1526, 419, 3491, 3019, 553, 3019, 13, 2055, 11, 74035, 31632, 1811, 220, 18, 6467, 389, 7014, 13, 5005, 566, 15804, 18