not right. Wait, no, that was adding 15 and 35, but maybe I confused something. Let me do it step by step again.\n\nCecile bought 15. Diane bought 20 more. So 20 more than Cecile means 15 + 20. So Diane has 35. Total is 15 (Cecile) + 35 (Diane) = 50. Yeah, that's right.\n\nAlternatively, could they have bought 15 apples in total? No, because the problem says Diane bought twenty more than Cecile. So if Cecile bought 15, Diane bought 35 total? So adding them makes 50. I think that's correct.\n\nWait, let me make sure there isn't a trick here. For example, sometimes problems can be ambiguous, but here it's straightforward. The key part is \"twenty more apples than Cecile.\" So if Cecile bought 15, then Diane has 15 + 20. And total is 15 + (15 + 20). Which is 15 + 15 + 20 = 30 + 20 = 50. Yep. That seems right.\n\nSo I think the answer is 50. All the steps check out. No mistakes here. So the total number of apples Diane and Cecile bought is 50.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of apples they bought altogether is \\boxed{50}.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, let's carefully analyze and break it down into manageable parts.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine how many apples Diane bought\n\nThe problem states:\n> Diane bought twenty more apples than Cecile.\n\nWe are told **Cecile bought 15 apples**.\n\nTherefore, the number of apples Diane bought is:\n$$\n15 \\text{ (Cecile's apples)} + 20 = 35\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the total number of apples bought by both persons\n\nWe now sum the number of apples both Cecile and Diane bought:\n$$\n15 \\text{ (Cecile's apples)} + 35 \\text{ (Diane's apples)} = 50\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{50}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Diane bought twenty more apples than Cecile. If Cecile bought 15 apples, how many apples did they buy altogether?", "answer": "Diane bought 15 + 20 = <<15+20=35>>35 apples.\nTherefore, they bought 15 + 35 = <<15+35=50>>50 apples altogether.\n#### 50", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Diane bought twenty more apples than Cecile. If Cecile bought 15 apples, how many apples did they buy altogether?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["50"], "solution": "50"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 53090, 10788, 17073, 803, 40676, 1091, 49145, 457, 13, 1416, 49145, 457, 10788, 220, 16, 20, 40676, 11, 1246, 1657, 40676, 1521, 807, 3695, 30055, 5267, 36842, 25, 151645, 198, 151644, 77091, 198], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 2055, 53090, 10788, 17073, 803, 40676, 1091, 49145, 457, 13, 1597, 49145, 457, 10788, 220, 16, 20, 40676, 13, 576, 3405, 374, 10161, 1246, 1657, 40676, 807, 10788, 30055, 13, 88190, 13, 97593, 11, 1077, 594, 1438, 419, 1495, 382, 5338, 11, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 40676, 53090, 10788, 13, 576, 3491, 2727]}
