 needs to buy a certain number of tanks, regardless of the capacity? But 500-liter tanks can only hold 500 liters each. But if he needs 10,000 liters, then he would need to buy 10,000 / 500 = 20 tanks.\n\nBut the solution might be presented as 20. But let me think again. Maybe the problem is that he starts with 500-liter tanks, but he needs to buy more because he needs to cover the balloons? Wait, but the problem says he \"buys 500-liter tanks of gas\" and asks how many tanks he needs to buy. Maybe it's a translation issue. Let me try to parse it again.\n\nOriginal problem: John buys 1000 balloons. Each balloon holds 10 liters of air. If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?\n\nSo, perhaps he needs to buy tanks that can hold enough gas to fill all the balloons, but he already has 500-liter tanks. Maybe the original problem was intending to have him buy tanks of specific capacities. But maybe the problem is straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is that he buys 500-liter tanks, and he needs exactly how many tanks? If each balloon requires some liters, then the number of tanks required is ( total atmospheric gas required ) / ( capacity of each tank ). \n\nSo total gas required is 1000 balloons multiplied by 10 liters each: 10,000 liters.\n\n500-liter tanks can hold 500 liters each. To find how many tanks are needed, divide 10,000 by 500, which is 20. Therefore, he needs to buy 20 tanks. \n\nIs that the answer? Let me check again.\n\nYes, because 500-liter tanks hold 500 liters each, and if he needs 10,000 liters, then 20 tanks would be 20*500 = 10,000 liters. Therefore, he needs to buy 20 tanks. \n\nBut why does he need to buy 20 tanks? Because he doesn't have the exact amount unless he buys the tanks. So since he already bought 500-liter tanks, it's just a matter of how many of those can hold the required gas. Since he needs 10,000 liters, which is 20 times 500 liters, he needs 20 tanks. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a trick here. Maybe the tanks are already designed to hold 500 liters, but he needs to buy more? But that doesn't make sense. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is in another language and \"buy 500-liter tanks\" is something else. For example, maybe he doesn't have tanks but needs to buy more. But the question says he does buy them, and asks how many tanks he needs to buy. So given that, the answer would be 20.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem says he pipes the gas into the balloons, but the question is about how many tanks he needs to buy to fill them. So regardless of how he gets the gas, he needs to get enough tanks. \n\nTherefore, the answer is 20.\n\n**Final Answer**\nJohn needs to buy \\boxed{20} tanks.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many 500-liter gas tanks John needs to purchase to fill **1000 balloons**, each holding **10 liters of gas**, we first calculate the total amount of gas required for all the balloons.\n\n- Number of balloons: 1000\n- Volume per balloon: 10 liters\n- Total gas required:  \n  $$\n  1000 \\text{ balloons} \\times 10 \\text{ liters} = 10,\\!000 \\text{ liters}\n  $$\n\nJohn has **500-liter tanks**, meaning he can use each tank for 500 liters of gas. However, he needs to cover the total gas requirement of 10,000 liters.\n\nTo determine the number of tanks required:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10,\\!000 \\text{ liters}}{500 \\text{ liters per tank}} = 20 \\text{ tanks}\n$$\n\nThus, John needs to **buy 20 tanks** to fill all the balloons.\n\n**Final Answer**: \\boxed{20}<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?", "answer": "He needs 1000*10=<<1000*10=10000>>10000 liters of gas\nSo he needs 10000/500=<<10000/500=20>>20 tanks\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 3757, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 220, 8886, 46756, 9982, 220, 16, 15, 92866, 315, 3720, 13, 220, 1416, 566, 49531, 220, 20, 15, 15, 85594, 25866, 315, 6819, 11, 1246, 1657, 25866, 1558], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 3757, 6801, 311, 3695, 69480, 323, 25866, 315, 6819, 311, 5155, 1105, 13, 6771, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 419, 700, 13, 4710, 5338, 11, 1077, 594, 53999, 279, 3491, 13, 3757, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 8886, 46756, 3880, 311, 3331, 220, 16, 15, 92866, 315, 3720, 13, 1260, 1083, 49531, 220, 20, 15, 15, 85594]}
