s per row. So multiplying those numbers: 3 rows * 20 seeds. That's 60 seeds per medium bed. Wait, 3 times 20 is 60. So each medium bed has 60 seeds.\n\nSince there are two medium beds, that would be 60 seeds per bed times 2 beds. So 120 seeds total for the medium beds. \n\nNow the large beds. The problem says there are two large beds. Each large bed can hold 4 rows with 25 seeds per row. So again, calculating per bed: 4 rows * 25 seeds. Which again is 100 seeds per large bed. Multiply by two for both beds: 100 seeds per bed * 2 = 200 seeds for two large beds.\n\nNow, adding all the seeds together: top bed (100) + medium beds (120) + large beds (200). Let me add those up.\n\n100 + 120 is 220. Then adding 200 gives 420. Wait, so total seeds is 420?\n\nBut let me make sure I didn't make any mistakes here. Let me recheck.\n\nTop bed: 4 rows * 25 seeds = 100. Correct. Medium bed: 3 rows * 20 seeds = 60. Two medium beds: 120. Large beds: 2 beds * 4 rows * 25 = 200. Yes. Total: 100 + 120 + 200 = 420. \n\nHmm, that seems right. Wait, but maybe I should check the multiplication again. For the large beds: 4 rows * 25 seeds is 100, and there are two large beds, so 100 * 2. Yes, 200. So that's correct. For the medium bed: 3 * 20 is indeed 60. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a different way to look at it? Let me think. The problem says the top bed can hold 4 rows with 25 seeds per row. So 4*25 is 100. The medium bed can hold 3 rows with 20 seeds per row, which is 3*20=60. Then multiplied by 2. And two large beds same as two medium beds? Wait, but the problem says there are two large beds on top and two medium beds on the bottom. So the total of four beds. \n\nYes, so adding all those numbers together gives 420. So the answer is 420 seeds. \n\nI think that's correct. Let me just verify once more. 4 rows * 25 seeds = 100. 3 rows * 20 seeds = 60. Two of each: 100 + 60 + 200 = 360. Wait, hold on! Wait, hold on. Wait, only the top bed is 100, the medium beds 120, and the large beds 