 of F. So J = (1/2)F + 10. Plugging in F = 24, we get J = (1/2)(24) + 10 = 12 + 10 = 22. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think in another way. If there are 10 more students in Johnson's class than half of Finley's, so half of Finley's is 12, plus 10 is 22. All checks out. \n\nI don't think I made any calculation errors here. So the answer should be 22. Let me just make sure that there isn't any trick or anything. The problem says \"half the number\", so 24 divided by 2 is 12. Then adding 10 gives 22. Yep, that seems straightforward. So I think that's the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we start by identifying the number of students in Mrs. Finley's class, which is given as **24 students**.\n\nHalf of Mrs. Finley's class is calculated as:\n$$\n\\frac{24}{2} = 12\n$$\n\nAccording to the problem, Mr. Johnson's class has **10 more** students than half the number in Mrs. Finley's class. Therefore, the number of students in Mr. Johnson's class is:\n$$\n12 + 10 = 22\n$$\n\nThus, the number of students in Mr. Johnson's class is $\\boxed{22}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Mrs. Finley's class has 24 students, and Mr. Johnson's class has 10 more than half the number in Mrs. Finley's class. How many students are in Mr. Johnson's class?", "answer": "Half the number in Mrs. Finley's class is 24/2=<<24/2=12>>12.\nMr. Johnson has 12+10=<<12+10=22>>22 students.\n#### 22", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Mrs. Finley's class has 24 students, and Mr. Johnson's class has 10 more than half the number in Mrs. Finley's class. How many students are in Mr. Johnson's class?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["22"], "solution": "22"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 17618, 13, 5649, 3179, 594, 536, 702, 220, 17, 19, 414